menu "Tab5 Pet Camera"

    config PETCAM_WIFI_SSID
        string "Wi-Fi SSID"
        default "myssid"
        help
            SSID of the 2.4 GHz network the Tab5 joins. The on-board ESP32-C6 is
            Wi-Fi 6 but 2.4 GHz only.

    config PETCAM_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "Wi-Fi password"
        default "mypassword"

    config PETCAM_WIFI_MAX_RETRY
        int "Wi-Fi connect retries before giving up"
        default 10

    config PETCAM_HOSTNAME
        string "mDNS hostname"
        default "tab5-petcam"
        help
            The camera is reachable at http://<hostname>.local/ from phones and
            laptops that speak mDNS (iOS and macOS always do; Android 12+ does).

    choice PETCAM_RESOLUTION
        prompt "Capture resolution"
        default PETCAM_RES_1280X720
        help
            MEASURED ON HARDWARE: the on-board SC202CS pipeline accepts only
            1280x720 in RGB565. Every other size here is rejected by
            VIDIOC_S_FMT, and the driver default (1280x720) is used instead —
            the firmware logs which sizes were accepted at startup.

            The other options are kept for a USB UVC camera, which negotiates
            its own set of sizes.

        config PETCAM_RES_640X480
            bool "640x480 (VGA)"
        config PETCAM_RES_800X600
            bool "800x600 (SVGA)"
        config PETCAM_RES_1280X720
            bool "1280x720 (HD)"
        config PETCAM_RES_1600X1200
            bool "1600x1200 (full sensor)"
    endchoice

    config PETCAM_WIDTH
        int
        default 640 if PETCAM_RES_640X480
        default 800 if PETCAM_RES_800X600
        default 1280 if PETCAM_RES_1280X720
        default 1600 if PETCAM_RES_1600X1200

    config PETCAM_HEIGHT
        int
        default 480 if PETCAM_RES_640X480
        default 600 if PETCAM_RES_800X600
        default 720 if PETCAM_RES_1280X720
        default 1200 if PETCAM_RES_1600X1200

    config PETCAM_JPEG_QUALITY
        int "JPEG quality (1-100)"
        range 1 100
        default 70
        help
            MEASURED at 1280x720 on this hardware: quality 80 produces ~170 KB
            per frame, which at 15 fps is ~20 Mbps — most of what the ESP32-C6
            link can carry. 70 leaves real headroom. Raise it if your network is
            quiet and you want the detail.

    config PETCAM_MAX_FPS
        int "Maximum frames per second to publish"
        range 1 30
        default 15
        help
            Frames arriving faster than this are dropped before encoding. 15 fps
            is plenty for watching a pet and halves the bandwidth versus 30.

    config PETCAM_PIXEL_REVERSE
        bool "Swap RGB565 byte order when encoding"
        default y
        help
            On. Without it the ISP's byte order reaches the JPEG encoder
            reversed: skies come out pink and brickwork cyan in the stream, in
            saved snapshots and in recorded clips. The panel is fed separately
            and looks right either way, which is what makes it easy to miss.

            Do not judge this by whether greens look green. Green occupies the
            middle six bits of RGB565 and survives the swap untouched — foliage
            and terminal text look perfectly normal while everything else is
            wrong. Look at a blue sky or red brick.

    config PETCAM_ENABLE_PREVIEW
        bool "Show the live camera image on the Tab5's own screen"
        default y

    config PETCAM_PREVIEW_FPS
        int "Preview refresh rate on the local screen"
        range 1 30
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_PREVIEW
        default 5
        help
            The preview competes with the network stream for memory bandwidth:
            every refresh pushes a full 720x1280 RGB565 frame to the panel. 5 fps
            is plenty to see a pet move and leaves the stream alone.

    config PETCAM_AUTO_ROTATE
        bool "Rotate the console with the device, using the BMI270"
        default y
        help
            The camera and the panel are fixed to the same body, so the live
            image keeps a constant relationship to the screen and only the text,
            QR code and buttons turn — the same behaviour as a phone camera app.

    config PETCAM_ORIENTATION_OFFSET
        int "Rotation offset for the upright pose (0, 90, 180 or 270)"
        range 0 270
        depends on PETCAM_AUTO_ROTATE
        default 0
        help
            MEASURED: holding the Tab5 landscape with the USB port on the left
            reports raw quadrant 90, which maps to LVGL rotation 90 and a
            1280x720 landscape logical screen — correct for that pose, so the
            offset stays 0.

            To recalibrate, hold the device the intended way and read
            orientation.raw from the /status endpoint.

    config PETCAM_CAMERA_MOUNT_ROTATION
        int "Sensor mounting correction in degrees (0, 90, 180 or 270)"
        range 0 270
        default 270
        help
            270 — the value BSP_CAMERA_ROTATION already gives for this board.

            Only 90 and 270 are geometrically valid. The camera is 16:9 and the
            panel is 9:16 in its native scan order, so an odd quarter-turn is
            what makes the image land exactly on the logical resolution in all
            four orientations; 0 and 180 would letterbox or overflow.

            The preview applies (this + screen rotation). It is a PLUS because
            the PPA rotates counter-clockwise while LVGL's display rotation runs
            the opposite way, so adding is what cancels the screen rotation and
            keeps the image fixed relative to the panel.

    config PETCAM_SPEAKER_VOLUME
        int "Speaker volume for played-back sounds (0-100)"
        range 0 100
        default 100
        help
            The Tab5's speaker is small and the sound has to carry across a room
            to a pet, so full volume is the useful default. Adjustable live at
            /volume?percent=N without a rebuild.

    config PETCAM_RECORD_COUNTDOWN_S
        int "Countdown shown on the Tab5 before recording starts"
        range 0 10
        default 3
        help
            The phone cannot tell you when the microphone opens, and the person
            who is about to speak is standing by the Tab5. The device counts
            down on its own screen so there is something to speak after.

    config PETCAM_RECORD_HOLD_MS
        int "How long the finished banner stays up, in milliseconds"
        range 0 3000
        default 1000
        help
            The banner reaching zero and the camera image reappearing in the
            same instant reads as the screen glitching rather than as the
            recording finishing. A pause on zero gives the sequence an end.
            Half a second was still too quick to register.

    config PETCAM_PLAYBACK_MAX_BOOST
        int "Largest amount playback may be amplified, in dB"
        range 0 24
        default 18
        help
            Recordings come off the microphone well below full scale — speech at
            a normal distance measured about -17 dBFS — so most of the amplifier
            never gets used. Before playing, the file is scanned for its loudest
            sample and scaled so that peak lands just under full scale.

            This matters more than the amplifier rating: 17 dB of unused
            headroom is far more than any setting on a 1 W speaker can recover.
            Raising the ceiling also raises the noise in quiet recordings, since
            the whole signal is scaled together.

    config PETCAM_MIC_CHANNEL
        int "Which of the codec's two inputs carries the microphone (0 or 1)"
        range 0 1
        default 1
        help
            Input 1. Recording speech through input 0 came out around -40 dBFS —
            a valid file that sounds like silence — while input 1 gives about
            -17 dBFS for the same speech.

            To check this, record while talking and look at the file, not at any
            per-channel meter: a four-channel probe of this codec reported levels
            that did not move when someone spoke and disagreed with what the
            capture path actually produced.

    config PETCAM_SCREEN_BRIGHTNESS
        int "Console screen brightness (0-100%)"
        range 0 100
        default 40
        help
            bsp_display_start() does NOT turn the backlight on — the BSP requires
            an explicit bsp_display_brightness_set(). Leave this at 0 and the
            panel stays dark even though LVGL is drawing correctly.

            40% is bright enough to read across a room and easier on a panel that
            is lit 24 hours a day.

    config PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        bool "Detect motion and record clips to the microSD card"
        default y

    config PETCAM_MOTION_SENSITIVITY
        int "Percent of the frame that must change to count as motion"
        range 1 100
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        default 8
        help
            Detection compares 40x24 brightness thumbnails a second apart. 8%
            catches a pet crossing the room while ignoring sensor noise and
            gradual light changes. Lower it if events are missed; raise it if
            shadows and auto-exposure keep triggering clips.

    config PETCAM_MOTION_PIXEL_DELTA
        int "Brightness change that marks a pixel as moved (0-255)"
        range 4 128
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        default 24
        help
            Raise this before raising the percentage if a still scene keeps
            triggering. In a dim room the sensor runs at high gain and the noise
            alone can move a quarter of the pixels by more than 24 levels.

            Check motion.score in /status with nothing moving: it should sit near
            zero. Whatever it settles at is your noise floor.

    config PETCAM_MOTION_LINGER_S
        int "Seconds of stillness before a clip is closed"
        range 1 120
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        default 10

    config PETCAM_MOTION_MAX_CLIP_S
        int "Maximum length of a single clip, in seconds"
        range 5 600
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        default 60
        help
            Stops one long-running disturbance — a curtain in a draught — from
            filling the card with a single enormous file. Continued motion simply
            starts the next clip.

    config PETCAM_CLIP_FPS
        int "Frames per second written into a clip"
        range 1 30
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        default 5
        help
            MEASURED: writing every encoded frame produced a 37 MB clip for one
            minute — about 2.2 GB per hour of activity, and a sustained 1.3 MB/s
            of card traffic. 5 fps is enough to follow what a pet did and cuts
            both by roughly two thirds.

    config PETCAM_CLIP_MIN_FREE_MB
        int "Free space to keep on the card, in MB"
        range 32 8192
        depends on PETCAM_ENABLE_MOTION
        default 512
        help
            Before each new clip, the oldest recordings are deleted until at
            least this much space is free. Without it an unattended camera simply
            stops recording the day the card fills, which is usually the day you
            most wanted the footage.

            At the default clip rate a minute of activity is roughly 20 MB, so
            512 MB leaves room for about 25 more minutes after a reclaim.

            The value is capped at a fifth of the card. A flat target larger than
            the volume can never be satisfied, and the reclaim loop would delete
            every recording trying to reach it.

    config PETCAM_TIMEZONE
        string "POSIX timezone for clip timestamps"
        default "JST-9"
        help
            Used to name clips in local time once SNTP has set the clock.
            "JST-9" is Japan; "GMT0BST,M3.5.0/1,M10.5.0" is the UK.

    config PETCAM_HTTP_PORT
        int "HTTP port for the page, snapshot and status"
        default 80

    config PETCAM_STREAM_PORT
        int "HTTP port for the MJPEG stream"
        default 81
        help
            The stream runs on its own server instance because an MJPEG response
            never ends, and a single esp_http_server instance handles requests
            one at a time — sharing a port would block the page from loading.

endmenu
